r gut health (4). What Is Thyroid Dysfunction? The thyroid gland is a butterfly-shaped organ located at the base of the neck, just below Adam’s apple. It produces hormones that regulate metabolism and energy levels in all parts of our body—including brain function (5). When it doesn't produce enough these essential chemical messengers called thyroid hormones T3 or T4 —or when they are not used properly by the cells, a condition known as hypothyroidism occurs. Symptoms can include fatigue and weight gain among others; however many people don’t realize that their symptoms may be related to an underactive gland (6). Hypothyroid disorders affect about five percent of postmenopausal women—and more than 10 million Americans suffer from some form or thyroid dysfunction. The condition can occur at any age, but it’s most common in older adults over the age of fifty years old; however many cases go undiagnosed because symptoms are often mistaken for other conditions like depression (7). Hypothyroidism is treatable with medication or dietary changes. Here's what you need to know about this condition that affects women in their 50’s and beyond—and how it may be linked to your gut health: What Are the Symptoms of Thyroid Dysfunction? The symptoms associated with hypothyroidism can vary widely from person-to-person. Some people experience no noticeable signs at all, while others suffer severe fatigue or weight gain (8). Here are some common indicators that your thyroid may not be functioning properly: Fatigue and sluggishness Constipation Dry skin Hair loss Memory problems Sensitivity to cold Muscle aches Depression Irritability Unexplained changes in menstrual cycle Increased cholesterol levels Hypothyroidism can occur at any age, but it’s most common among women over the age of fifty (9). The condition is treatable with medication or dietary adjustments. Here are some tips for managing your symptoms: Get tested if you experience several signs and/or have a family history Hypothyroidism can be caused by various factors, including autoimmune disorders like Hashimoto’s disease—which occurs when the body attacks its own thyroid gland (10). In fact, up to 95 percent of hypothyroid cases are due to this type of immune system dysfunction. Other causes include: Radiation therapy for cancer Treatment with certain medications such as lithium or amiodarone Surgical removal of all or part of the thyroid gland Iodine deficiency (10). The symptoms associated with hypothyroidism can vary widely from person-to-person. Some people experience no noticeable signs at all, while others suffer severe fatigue or weight gain—and these changes may not be immediately apparent to you as a result of aging alone; however many cases go undiagnosed because symptoms are often mistaken for other conditions like depression (10). Here’s what your doctor will look out-for when diagnosing this condition: Thyroid function tests—which measure levels in the bloodstream T3 and/or thyroxine, or “T4” hormones. These chemical messengers help regulate metabolism throughout our body (10). Ultrasound imaging of your neck to check for enlargement Hypothyroid disorders affect about five percent of postmenopausal women—and more than 10 million Americans suffer from some form or thyroid dysfunction. The condition can occur at any age, but it’s most common in older adults over the age fifty years old; however many cases go undiagnosed because symptoms are often mistaken for other conditions like depression (7). Hypothyroidism is treatable with medication or dietary changes—and here's what you need to know about this condition that affects women in their 50’s and beyond: What Are the Treatment Options? The most common treatment option involves taking synthetic thyroxine, a hormone replacement drug (1). This medication helps restore normal levels of T3 or “T4” chemical messengers to regulate metabolism throughout our body. It’s typically taken daily by mouth and can be adjusted as needed based on your doctor's recommendations; however some people may experience side effects like headaches, nausea—or even mood changes (1). Here are a few tips for managing these symptoms: Take medication with food to reduce stomach upset If you’re experiencing dry skin or hair loss consider using moisturizers and supplements that contain omega-3 fatty acids These essential fats help promote healthy cell membranes which can improve overall thyroid function (1). In addition, some studies suggest they may also reduce symptoms of hypothyroidism such as dry skin or hair loss; however more research is needed in this area. Here are a few ways to incorporate these beneficial fats into your diet: Eat fatty fish like salmon twice per week Add ground flaxseed meal (1 tablespoon daily) Use olive oil for cooking instead of butter or margarine Limit intake from other sources such as nuts and avocados These essential nutrients help promote healthy cell membranes which can improve overall thyroid function; however more research is needed in this area. Here are a few ways to incorporate these beneficial fats into your diet: Eat fatty fish like salmon twice per week Add ground flaxseed meal (1 tablespoon daily) Use olive oil for cooking instead of butter or margarine Limit intake from other sources such as nuts and avocados These essential nutrients help promote healthy cell membranes which can improve overall thyroid function; however more research is needed in this area. Here are a few ways to incorporate these beneficial fats into your diet: Eat fatty fish like salmon twice per week Add ground flaxseed meal (1 tablespoon daily) Use olive oil for cooking instead of butter or margarine Limit intake from other sources such as nuts and avocados These essential nutrients help promote healthy cell membranes which can improve overall thyroid function; however more research is needed in this area. Here are a few ways to incorporate these beneficial fats into your diet: Eat fatty fish like salm