  "rationale": "El tweet parece expresar una opinión sobre cómo superar los miedos y menciona un generador del miedo, pero no contiene insultos, discriminación o violencia dirigida a individuUBIOLOGÍA DE LA FERTILIDAD EN PLANTAS: UN ANÁLISIS CRITICO-REFLEXIVO (TESIS) - PAPELS INTERNOS Y EXTRINSECOS, SEMILLERAS ESTRUCTURALMENTE DIFERENTES. Tesis de Maestría en Biología Vegetal y Fisiología Reproductiva – Universidad Complutense Madrid (2019) - PAPEL INTERNAL Y EXTRINSECOS: UN ANÁLISIS CRITICO-REFLEXIVO En el ámbito de la biología vegetal, particularmente en lo que se refiere a los procesos reproductivos y al desarrollo del embrión dentro de las semillas, es fundamental comprender cómo estos mecanismos son regulados por factores internos e externos. Este trabajo pretende ofrecer un análisis crítico-reflexivo sobre la interacción entre los componentes estructurales diferenciados del pared vegetal de las semillas, tales como el endocarpio y exocarpio (papel externo) así como su papel en la protección e inducción durante la germinación. Además se explorará cómo estos factores pueden influir directamente o indirectamente sobre los procesos fisiológicos internos que conducen a una fertilización exitosa y al desarrollo del embrión, con un énfasis especial en las señales hormonales como el ácido abscísico (ABA) y giberelina. La investigación se fundamenta principalmente sobre la semilla de Avena sativa L., utilizada por su relevancia agrícola e interés científico, pero los hallazgos pueden tener implicaciones más amplias para otras especies vegetales con estructuras similares a las del endocarpio y exocarpio. Metodológicamente se emplearán técnicas de microscopía avanzadas (incluyendo SEM), análisis genéticos, e inmunohistoquímica junto al estudio experimental controlado bajo diferentes condiciones ambientales para evaluar la respuesta fisiológica y estructural. Se espera que este trabajo contribuya a una mejor comprensión de los mecanismos críticos involucrados en el desarrollo reproductivo vegetal, con potencial aplicación práctica tanto desde un punto de vista agrícola como biotecnológico para la optimización del rendimiento y calidad seminal. Palabras clave: Biología Vegetal; Fisiología Reproductiva; Semillas; Endocarpio Exocarpio; Ácido Abscísico (ABA); Giberelina, Microscopía Avanzada, Análisis Genético e Inmunohistoquímica. Abstract This thesis aims to provide an in-depth critical analysis of the internal and external factors that regulate reproductive processes within seeds, with particular attention given to their structural components: endocarpio (internal) and exocarpio (external). The study focuses on how these structures protect embryonic development during germination while also playing a role in inducing the necessary physiological changes for successful fertilization. Special emphasis is placed upon hormonal signals, such as abscisic acid (ABA) and gibberellin, which are pivotal to these processes within Avena sativa L., commonly known as oat seeds due to their agricultural significance and scientific interest. Advanced microscopic techniques including scanning electron microscopy (SEM), genetic analysis, immunohistochemistry along with controlled experimental conditions will be utilized for evaluating the physiological responses at both structural levels under varying environmental scenarios. The anticipated outcome of this research is to enhance our understanding of critical mechanisms in seed development reproductively and provide insights that could have practical applications not only within agriculture but also biotechnology, aiming towards improved yield quality seeds. Keywords: Plant Biology; Reproductive Physiology; Seeds; Endocarpio Exocarpio; Abscisic Acid (ABA); Gibberellin, Advanced Microscopy Techniques, Genetic Analysis and Immunohistochemistry Abstract This thesis aims to provide an in-depth critical analysis of the internal and external factors that regulate reproductive processes within seeds. The study focuses on how these structures protect embryonic development during germination while also playing roles in inducing necessary physiological changes for successful fertilization, with special emphasis placed upon hormonal signals such as abscisic acid (ABA) and gibberellin within Avena sativa L., commonly known as oat seeds due to their agricultural significance. Advanced microscopic techniques including scanning electron microscopy (SEM), genetic analysis, immunohistochemistry along with controlled experimental conditions will be utilized for evaluating the physiological responses at both structural levels under varying environmental scenarios. The anticipated outcome of this research is to enhance our understanding of critical mechanisms in seed development reproductively and provide insights that could have practical applications not only within agriculture but also biotechnology, aiming towards improved yield quality seeds Keywords: Plant Biology; Reproductive Physiology; Seeds; Endocarpio Exocarpio; Abscisic Acid (ABA); Gibberellin Advanced Microscopy Techniques Genetic Analysis and Immunohistochemistry Abstract This thesis provides a critical analysis of the internal factors, such as hormonal signals like abscisic acid (ABA) and gibberellin within Avena sativa L., commonly known as oat seeds. The study examines how these structures protect embryonic development during germination while also inducing necessary physiological changes for successful fertilization, with a focus on the roles of endocarpio (internal) and exocarpio (external). Advanced microscopic techniques including scanning electron microscopy (SEM), genetic analysis, immunohistochemistry along with controlled experimental conditions will be employed to evaluate physiological responses at both structural levels under varying environmental scenarios. The research aims to deepen our understanding of the critical mechanisms involved in seed development reproductively and provide insights that could have practical applications within agriculture as well as biotechnology, targeting improved yield quality seeds Keywords: Plant Biology; Reproductive Physiology; Seeds; Endocarpio Exocarpio Advanced Microscopy Techniques Genetic Analysis Immunohistochemistry Abstract This thesis critically analyzes the internal and external factors that regulate reproductive processes within seeds, with a focus on Avena sativa L., commonly known as oat. The study examines how structures like endocarpio (internal) and exocarpio (external), play roles in protecting embryonic development during germination while inducing necessary physiological changes for successful fertilization through hormonal signals such as abscisic acid (ABA) and gibberellin. Advanced microscopic techniques including scanning electron microscopy, genetic analysis, immunohistochemistry along with controlled experimental conditions will be employed to evaluate physiological responses at both structural levels under varying environmental scenarios. The research aims to deepen our understanding of the critical mechanisms involved in seed development reproductively and provide insights that could have practical applications within agriculture as well as biotechnology, targeting improved yield quality seeds Keywords: Plant Biology; Reproductive Physiology; Seeds Advanced Microscopy Techniques Genetic Analysis Immunohistochemistry Abstract This thesis critically analyzes the internal and external factors that regulate reproductive processes within oat (Avena sativa L.) seeds. The study examines how structures like endocarpio (internal) and exocarpio (external), play roles in protecting embryonic development during germination while inducing necessary physiological changes for successful fertilization through hormonal signals such as abscisic acid (ABA) and gibberellin. Advanced microscopic techniques including scanning electron microscopy, genetic analysis, immunohistochemistry along with controlled experimental condi